{"answer":"No. For the permitted input n=40,\n\nn^2+n+41=40^2+40+41=1681=41·41.\n\nSince 1<41<1681, this value is composite. Therefore the statement is false.","approaches":[{"description":"Refute the universal assertion with one nonnegative input for which the polynomial is composite.","id":"factor-counterexample","outcome":"candidate","reason":"n=40 yields the explicit nontrivial factorization 1681=41·41."},{"description":"Infer universal primality from initial values of the polynomial.","id":"initial-pattern","outcome":"rejected","reason":"Finitely many positive instances cannot establish a statement about every nonnegative integer, and n=40 is a counterexample."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"\"Is n^2+n+41 prime for every nonnegative integer n?\"","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"question-domain","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The proposed assertion concerns n^2+n+41 for every nonnegative integer n.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Direct substitution and arithmetic in evaluate-forty.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"value-at-forty","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"At n=40, n^2+n+41=1681.","step_ids":["evaluate-forty"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The explicit factorization in factor-value gives a divisor strictly between 1 and 1681.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["value-at-forty"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"composite-at-forty","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The value n^2+n+41 is composite at n=40.","step_ids":["factor-value"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Applied the counterexample rule in refute-universal.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["question-domain","composite-at-forty"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"universal-claim-false","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"It is false that n^2+n+41 is prime for every nonnegative integer n.","step_ids":["refute-universal"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"evaluate-forty","justification":"40 is nonnegative, and direct arithmetic gives 40^2+40+41=1600+40+41=1681.","statement":"Evaluate the polynomial at the permitted input n=40."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["evaluate-forty"],"id":"factor-value","justification":"1681=41·41 and 1<41<1681, so 1681 has a nontrivial positive divisor and is composite.","statement":"Establish that the value at n=40 is composite."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["evaluate-forty","factor-value"],"id":"refute-universal","justification":"A universal assertion fails if a member of its stated domain fails the asserted property. Here n=40 is in the domain and its value is composite, not prime.","statement":"Use n=40 as a counterexample to the universal assertion."}],"question_status":"refuted","tool_requests":[]}
